  ],
  "opening": "In Chapter 6, we saw that applying a reversible circuit \\(U_f\\) to a superposition of inputs evaluates \\(f(x)\\) on every input at once, yet measuring the register reveals only one random pair \\((x, f(x))\\). To extract information about the function as a whole, the computational branches must interfere before measurement.",
  "opening_continuation": "We first examine how Hadamard gates can recombine branches to extract global properties and diagnose why target entanglement destroys interference for non-constant functions. We then formalize query access and show how phase kickback records function values in relative phases without entangling the target register, yielding our first quantum algorithm.",
  "intro_continuation": "Classically, finding out whether \\(f(0) = f(1)\\) requires evaluating \\(f\\) twice\u2014once on \\(0\\) and once on \\(1\\)\u2014because knowing \\(f(0)\\) alone leaves both possibilities open. Can a quantum computation answer this question using only a single evaluation of \\(U_f\\)?"
}